Z||000000||Eric||06-03-99||03:54 PM||||What parts are involved in a typical clutch repair?  I want to replace everything in question and not leave any "maybes" or "mightlasts".  I know I have to drop the engine for this job, so would it be in my best interest to rebuild the engine at the same time even though I don't have all the parts on hand for my 3.2 rebuild?  I'm worried it too might have a sudden death like the clutch (140,000 miles).  <p>To some of you guys with a 3.2 rebuild: what is the best clutch for this application?  How much can I expect to spend?  I was considering going with the Stage 1 Centerforce clutch with 30% more holding power, but I'm not sure if it would work as well as the Dual Friction.  Any input would be appreciated.
Z||000001||Rob||06-03-99||08:40 PM||||You don't need to take the motor out to change the clutch, just lower it about four inches. The clutch I put in my car is the Centerforce, it cost about $500.00 for a matched set. Also $40.00 to have my flywheel resurfaced. It took me about 10 hours to do all the work but I had to wait overnight for the flywheel. This setup works great!   
Z||000002||Carrolles||06-04-99||06:49 AM||||Please give details of your method of replacing the clutch without completely removing the engine/cradle.<p>I've got a clutch changeout waiting for me on my 85SE v6 4spd.<p>Thanks,<br>Carroll
